Why This Is Asked

Tests practical multi-tenant isolation, per-tenant offset management, and resilience in KCNA with simple backpressure and testing.

Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation and routing
- Token-bucket rate limiting per tenant
- Durable per-tenant offsets and recovery
- Idempotent processing with robust retries
- Lightweight test harness simulating downstream lag

Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: per-tenant rate limiter and offset store
class TenantState {
  constructor(tenant, rate) {
    this.tenant = tenant;
    this.offsets = new Map(); // topic -> offset
    this.limiter = new TokenBucket(rate); // per-tenant rate
  }
  canConsume() { return this.limiter.tryConsume(this.tenant); }
  commitOffset(topic, offset) { this.offsets.set(topic, offset); }
}
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate) {
    this.rate = rate; this.tokens = rate; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.rate, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you persist offsets across restarts and recover after crash?
- How would you scale the rate limiter across many tenants?
- What metrics would you collect to detect backlog per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a common real-world challenge in Kafka streaming: handling evolving event schemas. It tests understanding of versioned schemas, backward/forward compatibility, data transformation, multi-sink routing with threshold-based logic, and idempotent sinks with per-message deduplication.

## Key Concepts
- **Versioned schemas and backward/forward compatibility**: Handling schema evolution without breaking consumers
- **Data transformation and normalization**: Converting multiple schema versions into a unified structure
- **Idempotent sinks and per-message deduplication**: Ensuring exactly-once semantics using deduplication
- **Multi-sink routing**: Directing events to different destinations based on business logic

## Code Example
```javascript
class VersionedConsumer {
  constructor(threshold, sinks) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.sinks = sinks;
    this.seenIds = new LRUCache(10000);
  }

  process(message) {
    const msgId = message.messageId;
    if (this.seenIds.has(msgId)) return; // skip duplicates

    const record = this.normalize(message);
    const sink = record.value > this.threshold ? this.sinks.alerts : this.sinks.default;
    sink.write(record);
    this.seenIds.add(msgId);
  }

  normalize(msg) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(msg.payload);
    return {
      sensorId: obj.sensorId,
      value: obj.value,
      timestamp: obj.timestamp,
      location: obj.version === 2 ? obj.location : null,
      version: obj.version
    };
  }
}
```

## Canary Test
```javascript
const consumer = new VersionedConsumer(100, { alerts: mockSink(), default: mockSink() });
const v1 = { messageId: '1', payload: JSON.stringify({ version: 1, sensorId: 's1', value: 50, timestamp: 1000 }) };
const v2 = { messageId: '1', payload: JSON.stringify({ version: 2, sensorId: 's2', value: 150, timestamp: 1001, location: 'A' }) };

consumer.process(v1);
consumer.process(v2);
assert(defaultSink.calls === 1);
assert(alertsSink.calls === 1);
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests stateful per-key windowing, dynamic rule handling, and exactly-once guarantees under late messages.

## Key Concepts
- Per-account sliding window sums
- Dynamic rule lookup and on-the-fly threshold changes
- Watermarks for late arrivals and eviction
- Exactly-once semantics and idempotent fraud_alerts
- Backpressure and test canary design

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: per-account rolling sum with 5m sliding window
state = Map(); // accountId -> [{ts, amt}]
rules = Map(); // accountId -> threshold
onEvent(tx) {
  cleanExpired(tx.ts);
  sum = sumAmt(state.get(tx.accountId));
  threshold = rules.get(tx.accountId) ?? default;
  if (sum > threshold) emitFraud(tx.accountId, sum, threshold);
  append(state, tx.accountId, {ts: tx.ts, amt: tx.amount});
}
```

